WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:38.861
[Music]

00:00:38.861 --> 00:00:44.181
[Narrator:] This man has just spent ten months, three weeks, and one day in this hospital.

00:00:44.181 --> 00:00:46.901
Today he has been discharged.

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:49.521
The disease he had was tuberculosis.

00:00:49.521 --> 00:00:56.401
The man's name is Edward R. Evans, and the woman getting out of the car to meet him is his wife, Martha.

00:00:59.301 --> 00:01:04.081
For her, this has been ten months, three weeks, and one day of waiting.

00:01:04.441 --> 00:01:07.141
She wonders if their marriage will ever be the same.

00:01:07.141 --> 00:01:13.201
[Music

00:01:13.241 --> 00:01:17.261
[Car engine starting]

00:01:18.121 --> 00:01:21.801
[Ed Evans:] Oh, this is the best meal I've had in months.

00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:24.381
Martha, when it comes to pot roast, you are a genius.

00:01:24.381 --> 00:01:29.421
[Martha Evans:] Glad you enjoyed it, Ed, but tonight all the credit really belongs to your daughter.

00:01:29.421 --> 00:01:31.661
[Ed Evans:] Dorothy? Well, last I remember

00:01:31.661 --> 00:01:34.901
you wouldn't even set foot inside the kitchen, hated to boil eggs.

00:01:34.901 --> 00:01:37.741
[Dorothy Evans:] That was before I took home economics in school.

00:01:37.741 --> 00:01:40.161
You won't believe it, but I like to cook now.

00:01:40.161 --> 00:01:42.201
[Ed Evans:] Will miracles never end?

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:45.961
Hey, Martha, still think you can win a game of canasta from me?

00:01:45.961 --> 00:01:47.961
[Martha Evans:] Well, we'll find out, soon as I clear away.

00:01:47.961 --> 00:01:49.721
[Dorothy Evans:] Don't bother, Mom. I'll do it.

00:01:49.721 --> 00:01:51.581
[Martha Evans:] Well, if you don't mind.

00:01:51.581 --> 00:01:54.361
Oh, wasn't Johnny coming over to study with you tonight?

00:01:54.361 --> 00:01:56.081
[Dorothy Evans:] He couldn't make it.

00:01:56.081 --> 00:01:56.821
You go on, Mom.

00:01:56.821 --> 00:01:58.561
I'll be careful with the glasses.

00:01:58.561 --> 00:02:00.101
[Martha Evans:] All right, dear.

00:02:02.801 --> 00:02:04.481
Come on, Mr. Evans.

00:02:04.481 --> 00:02:07.301
[Ed Evans:] I warn you I've had a lot of time to practice.

00:02:07.301 --> 00:02:10.241
Things are going to be a lot different from now on.

00:02:10.761 --> 00:02:14.221
[Dorothy Evans, thinking to herself:] I guess everything will be different from now on.

00:02:14.221 --> 00:02:17.301
Johnny knows why Dad's been in the hospital.

00:02:17.301 --> 00:02:20.181
Maybe he won't come over anymore.

00:02:20.181 --> 00:02:27.221
I'm sure Dad's cured, not dangerous or anything, but will Johnny understand that?

00:02:27.721 --> 00:02:31.121
Suppose his folks tell him to stop seeing me?

00:02:31.121 --> 00:02:35.161
Maybe I'll even have to quit school and get a job to help out.

00:02:35.161 --> 00:02:36.801
Why?

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:39.761
Why did this have to happen to us?

00:02:40.861 --> 00:02:45.901
[Martha Evans:] Ed, will you have much trouble in getting an office job at the plant?

00:02:45.901 --> 00:02:47.641
[Ed Evans:] Why should I?

00:02:47.641 --> 00:02:49.541
I've been with the Martin Company as long as anybody.

00:02:49.541 --> 00:02:51.241
I know the business backwards.

00:02:51.241 --> 00:02:55.701
[Martha Evans:] Yes, dear, but as foreman your experience has all been in the shop.

00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:57.981
[Ed Evans:] Martha, I know you can't help worrying about it.

00:02:57.981 --> 00:03:02.721
Our savings are almost gone, and well, I'm sure it hasn't been easy for you and Dorothy.

00:03:02.721 --> 00:03:05.641
So I can't boss the gang at the furniture plant,

00:03:05.651 --> 00:03:09.931
but I can still put in my eight-hour day and earn my pay as well as anybody else.

00:03:09.931 --> 00:03:13.931
And don't forget that studying course the hospital recommended.

00:03:13.931 --> 00:03:17.931
Those hours I put in studying bookkeeping are going to come in mighty handy.

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:21.171
I may not be a CPA yet, but it's a place to start.

00:03:21.171 --> 00:03:23.551
It'll be all right, dear, I know it will.

00:03:23.551 --> 00:03:26.011
[Martha Evans:] Of course it will, Ed.

00:03:26.011 --> 00:03:27.821
Let's finish the game.

00:03:27.831 --> 00:03:29.221
It's your play.

00:03:29.221 --> 00:03:29.861
[Ed Evans:] Oh.

00:03:29.861 --> 00:03:37.541
[Martha, thinking to herself:] Will it really be all right after all this time and all the money we've had to spend?

00:03:37.541 --> 00:03:41.221
Well, all we can do is hope.

00:03:41.261 --> 00:03:45.521
[Music]

00:03:45.521 --> 00:03:48.341
[Mr. Wilson:] Of course we're delighted to see you back on your feet again.

00:03:48.341 --> 00:03:50.301
The TB people said you'd be released soon.

00:03:50.301 --> 00:03:52.061
[Ed Evans:] They tell me I'm as good as new.

00:03:52.061 --> 00:03:53.881
[Mr. Wilson:] Oh, that's fine, fine.

00:03:53.881 --> 00:03:56.081
It's a real shame you can't go back into the shop.

00:03:56.081 --> 00:03:57.901
We really could use you there.

00:03:57.901 --> 00:04:00.981
[Ed Evans:] Well, I still know the furniture business, Mr. Wilson,

00:04:00.981 --> 00:04:04.801
and as I've told you, I've been studying up on bookkeeping methods in the hospital.

00:04:04.801 --> 00:04:07.221
[Mr. Wilson:] Yes, yes, I know.

00:04:07.221 --> 00:04:09.621
I really wish I could do something for you.

00:04:12.581 --> 00:04:15.301
[Mr. Wilson, thinking to himself:] What do you do in a case like this?

00:04:15.301 --> 00:04:17.901
I could find a job for him, sure...

00:04:17.901 --> 00:04:21.121
then it's on my shoulders if anything goes wrong.

00:04:21.421 --> 00:04:23.541
Suppose he gets sick again?

00:04:23.921 --> 00:04:28.981
Suppose his TB is still contagious and some of the other men get it?

00:04:28.981 --> 00:04:31.741
Oh, I can't be responsible for him.

00:04:32.901 --> 00:04:36.021
[Mr. Wilson:] The trouble is, Ed, we're overloaded on our office staff now,

00:04:36.021 --> 00:04:39.681
and I'd have to let somebody else go to fit you in, you see?

00:04:39.681 --> 00:04:42.041
[Ed Evans:] I understand.

00:04:42.041 --> 00:04:42.901
[Mr. Wilson:] I'm sorry, Ed.

00:04:42.901 --> 00:04:44.981
Perhaps later if there's an opening?

00:04:44.981 --> 00:04:48.261
[Ed Evans:] Well, thank you very much, Mr. Wilson.

00:04:48.261 --> 00:04:52.221
[Mr. Wilson:] If you want references or anything like that, I'll be glad to give them to you anytime.

00:04:52.221 --> 00:04:53.241
[Ed Evans:] That's very kind of you.

00:04:53.241 --> 00:04:54.041
Thanks a lot.

00:04:56.141 --> 00:04:57.761
[Mr. Wilson:] Good luck.

00:04:59.981 --> 00:05:06.901
[Music]

00:05:06.901 --> 00:05:09.681
[Woman reviewing job application:] So far so good, Mr. Evans.

00:05:09.681 --> 00:05:19.081
[Music]

00:05:19.081 --> 00:05:23.641
Well, I think that gives me a pretty complete picture of your employment background.

00:05:23.641 --> 00:05:27.781
Unfortunately, I don't have anything in your line right at the moment.

00:05:27.781 --> 00:05:31.841
However, I'll certainly keep your application on file, and if anything does come in...

00:05:31.841 --> 00:05:34.421
[Ed Evans:] I see. Thank you very much.

00:05:34.421 --> 00:05:38.801
[Music]

00:05:38.801 --> 00:05:40.101
What's the use?

00:05:40.101 --> 00:05:42.301
I fill them out and they file them.

00:05:42.301 --> 00:05:43.761
Thank you, Mr. Evans.

00:05:43.761 --> 00:05:46.121
We'll call you if something comes up.

00:05:46.121 --> 00:05:50.481
If something doesn't come up soon, Martha, I don't know what we're going to do.

00:05:50.481 --> 00:05:56.421
[Martha Evans:] I wish I knew what to tell you, Ed, but you've just going to keep trying.

00:05:56.421 --> 00:06:00.661
[Ed Evans:] Yeah. Well, maybe this will be the lucky one.

00:06:02.741 --> 00:06:05.801
[Mr. Verity:] We've been doing business with the Martin Furniture company for a good many years.

00:06:05.801 --> 00:06:08.821
Anybody with them as long as you were comes pretty highly recommended.

00:06:08.821 --> 00:06:10.001
[Ed Evans:] Glad to hear it, sir.

00:06:10.001 --> 00:06:14.461
[Mr. Verity:] Besides, I appreciate what you're up against, learning something new, starting all over again.

00:06:14.461 --> 00:06:17.001
I had to do it myself about 15 years ago.

00:06:17.001 --> 00:06:18.761
Let's stop in here a minute.

00:06:18.761 --> 00:06:21.381
I'll introduce you to the man you'll be working under.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:26.081
Oh, by the way, I don't see any real reason for telling any of the boys in here

00:06:26.081 --> 00:06:27.981
what you've been doing the last year, do you?

00:06:27.981 --> 00:06:29.361
[Ed Evans:] No, not if you don't, Mr. Verity.

00:06:29.361 --> 00:06:30.201
[Mr. Verity:] Good.

00:06:32.641 --> 00:06:33.441
Bill.

00:06:33.861 --> 00:06:35.381
Meet Ed Evans.

00:06:35.381 --> 00:06:37.881
Ed, this is Bill Keating.

00:06:37.881 --> 00:06:38.721
[Bill:] How do you do?

00:06:38.721 --> 00:06:39.221
[Ed Evans:] Hello.

00:06:39.221 --> 00:06:41.841
[Mr. Verity:] He's in charge of the department, and you'll be working directly under him.

00:06:41.841 --> 00:06:43.361
[Bill:] Sure, nice to meet you, Mr. Evans.

00:06:43.361 --> 00:06:44.681
[Ed Evans:] Same here.

00:06:44.681 --> 00:06:46.801
[Bill:] Say, don't I know you from somewhere?

00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:48.041
Evans?

00:06:48.561 --> 00:06:51.721
Why, sure, you used to belong to our Legion Post.

00:06:51.721 --> 00:06:53.781
[Ed Evans:] Of course, I remember you, too.

00:06:53.781 --> 00:06:55.781
You led the drill team one year.

00:06:55.791 --> 00:06:56.731
[Bill:] That's right.

00:06:56.731 --> 00:06:58.491
Say, I haven't seen you around for a while.

00:06:58.491 --> 00:06:59.831
Must be almost a year.

00:06:59.831 --> 00:07:01.241
Have you been out of town?

00:07:01.251 --> 00:07:04.391
[Mr. Verity:] Bill, you can introduce Ed to the rest of the boys a little later.

00:07:04.391 --> 00:07:07.061
Right now I want to take him around and show him the rest of our place.

00:07:07.071 --> 00:07:08.851
[Bill:] Fine, Mr. Verity. See you later, Evans.

00:07:08.851 --> 00:07:10.061
[Ed Evans:] All right.

00:07:16.061 --> 00:07:20.701
[Bill, thinking to himself:] Ed Evans. There's something else connected with that name.

00:07:20.701 --> 00:07:22.381
Sure. I remember.

00:07:22.381 --> 00:07:25.361
The Legion Post sent him flowers in the hospital.

00:07:25.361 --> 00:07:27.941
He had tuberculosis...

00:07:27.941 --> 00:07:31.021
and now he figures to sit at the desk next to mine.

00:07:34.681 --> 00:07:40.101
Listen fellas, I don't know how you feel about it, but personally I think we've got a little problem here.

00:07:40.781 --> 00:07:43.821
That fellow that just left with Mr. Verity....

00:07:43.821 --> 00:07:45.601
[Martha Evans:] But I don't understand you, Ed.

00:07:45.601 --> 00:07:51.001
Mr. Verity told you the job was yours, and just because this fellow recognized you from the Legion...

00:07:51.001 --> 00:07:52.961
[Ed Evans:] I told you it wasn't that, Martha.

00:07:52.961 --> 00:07:55.481
You should have seen the look on his face.

00:07:55.481 --> 00:07:58.581
I remember that look often enough these past few months.

00:07:58.581 --> 00:08:00.221
I could tell what he was thinking.

00:08:00.221 --> 00:08:03.021
He remembered that I was in the hospital and why.

00:08:03.021 --> 00:08:04.061
[Martha Evans:] Ed, please.

00:08:04.061 --> 00:08:06.301
You're just getting yourself all worked up.

00:08:06.301 --> 00:08:08.261
Suppose we take in a movie after dinner?

00:08:08.261 --> 00:08:11.341
[Ed Evans:] Don't we ever go out with our old friends anymore?

00:08:11.341 --> 00:08:14.361
I thought you were going to call Bill and Jane this morning.

00:08:14.361 --> 00:08:18.881
[Martha Evans:] Well, I did, but Jane said their boy was down with a cold.

00:08:18.881 --> 00:08:21.601
[Ed Evans:] I wouldn't believe it if I read it in a book.

00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:23.561
In this day and age...

00:08:23.561 --> 00:08:25.641
[Martha Evans:] What are you talking about, Ed?

00:08:25.641 --> 00:08:29.361
[Ed Evans:] The Warrens last week, Joe and Betsy Kimball the week before...

00:08:29.361 --> 00:08:33.681
a remarkable number of our so-called friends seem to be seeing less and less of us

00:08:33.681 --> 00:08:35.021
since I've been home from the hospital.

00:08:35.021 --> 00:08:38.241
[Martha Evans:] Ed, you're just adding up things that don't exist.

00:08:38.241 --> 00:08:38.941
[Ed Evans:] Am I?

00:08:38.941 --> 00:08:43.141
Nobody has really come out and said so, but people are afraid of me, Martha.

00:08:43.141 --> 00:08:44.721
[Martha Evans:] But they told you at the hospital

00:08:44.721 --> 00:08:47.681
there'd be a certain number of people who would always be...

00:08:49.981 --> 00:08:52.441
[Dorothy Evans:] Gee, you're still here.

00:08:52.441 --> 00:08:54.721
I thought you were going out tonight.

00:08:54.721 --> 00:08:56.421
You said you were going to the Ellsworths.

00:08:56.421 --> 00:08:57.421
[Martha Evans:] It's been called off.

00:08:57.421 --> 00:08:59.361
[Ed Evans:] Does it make any difference?

00:08:59.361 --> 00:09:05.241
[Dorothy Evans:] No. Only I invited Johnny and some of the gang over a little later, but I can still phone...

00:09:05.241 --> 00:09:06.961
[Ed Evans:] Wait a minute, Dorothy.

00:09:06.961 --> 00:09:08.601
What are you going to phone them about?

00:09:08.601 --> 00:09:10.961
[Dorothy Evans:] Well, if you're staying home,

00:09:10.961 --> 00:09:13.821
I thought we'd better go to Johnny's house or one of the other kids.

00:09:13.821 --> 00:09:16.701
[Martha Evans:] Well, you don't have to do that.

00:09:16.701 --> 00:09:18.421
We don't mind having them over.

00:09:23.481 --> 00:09:26.781
[Dorothy Evans:] How can I tell them that I don't want to ask the gang over?

00:09:26.781 --> 00:09:30.841
How I'm afraid they won't come, because of dad?

00:09:30.841 --> 00:09:34.121
[Ed Evans:] You don't want them to come over because of me.

00:09:38.361 --> 00:09:43.041
Dorothy, I can remember when your friends used to swarm all over this house.

00:09:43.041 --> 00:09:44.741
[Dorothy Evans:] They're older, dad.

00:09:44.751 --> 00:09:46.491
Not the same ones you remember.

00:09:46.491 --> 00:09:50.031
[Ed Evans:] Well, this Johnny I keep hearing about, I've never met him yet.

00:09:50.031 --> 00:09:54.461
Dorothy, are you afraid that you might get sick, might catch tuberculosis because of me?

00:09:54.461 --> 00:09:55.521
[Dorothy Evans:] No.

00:09:55.521 --> 00:09:58.781
[Ed Evans:] Then you know that I wouldn't have been released if there was the slightest chance...

00:09:58.781 --> 00:10:02.401
[Dorothy Evans:] I know it, but some of my friends don't.

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:04.941
[Ed Evans:] Then why don't you tell them?

00:10:04.941 --> 00:10:09.901
[Dorothy Evans:] I tried to at first, but we got into an argument,

00:10:09.901 --> 00:10:12.901
and I was afraid they wouldn't believe me anyway.

00:10:12.901 --> 00:10:17.161
After that I made up my mind to avoid the whole subject,

00:10:17.161 --> 00:10:20.701
and as long as they didn't come over while you were here....

00:10:20.701 --> 00:10:23.901
Well, I didn't know what else to do.

00:10:23.901 --> 00:10:25.961
[Ed Evans:] I understand.

00:10:25.961 --> 00:10:30.761
Believe me, I do, and I'm sorry I haven't tried to help you on your problems before,

00:10:30.761 --> 00:10:35.141
but you see now that avoiding the subject is really no answer at all, don't you?

00:10:35.141 --> 00:10:38.461
Then why don't you go upstairs and fix up your face, and when your friends come,

00:10:38.461 --> 00:10:42.421
if there are any arguments to be settled at all, maybe I can sort of act as referee.

00:10:42.421 --> 00:10:44.461
[Dorothy Evans:] All right, dad.

00:10:44.461 --> 00:10:46.481
Don't you worry about me.

00:10:46.481 --> 00:10:47.801
I'll be fine now.

00:10:47.801 --> 00:10:49.061
[Ed Evans:] Okay.

00:10:51.361 --> 00:10:56.121
[Martha Evans:] You know, Ed, that was awfully good advice you gave Dorothy.

00:10:56.121 --> 00:11:01.421
I'm just wondering if you shouldn't try some of your own medicine on that Keating fellow at the lumber company.

00:11:01.421 --> 00:11:03.841
[Ed Evans:] That's funny.

00:11:03.841 --> 00:11:06.441
I was just thinking the same thing.

00:11:07.361 --> 00:11:10.241
[Ed, back in his new office:] That's about all I can say.

00:11:10.241 --> 00:11:12.021
I'm completely cured.

00:11:12.021 --> 00:11:15.581
As a matter of fact, I'm really safer to be near than you are,

00:11:15.581 --> 00:11:19.281
because I have to keep going back to the hospital for my regular checkups.

00:11:19.281 --> 00:11:22.001
There's absolutely no danger of infection.

00:11:22.001 --> 00:11:24.381
My doctors told Mr. Verity that.

00:11:24.381 --> 00:11:29.041
Except for the fact that I need more rest than most people, I'm no different than any of you.

00:11:33.921 --> 00:11:37.161
[Bill:] Bill Keating, will you ever learn?

00:11:37.161 --> 00:11:42.161
There you were, all ready to strike out a man before he ever got a chance to go to bat, and why?

00:11:42.161 --> 00:11:44.141
Because you didn't stop to think.

00:11:44.141 --> 00:11:47.581
You listened to rumors and jumped to conclusions, that's why.

00:11:51.381 --> 00:11:54.261
Evans, I think I owe you an apology.

00:11:54.261 --> 00:11:56.981
I didn't have all the facts straight, but I do now,

00:11:56.981 --> 00:12:01.041
and as far as I'm concerned, I'll see that you get every break possible.

00:12:01.041 --> 00:12:06.021
[Ed Evans:] I'm not asking for any special breaks, just a fair chance, that's all.

00:12:07.001 --> 00:12:10.781
[Narrator:] And Ed Evans got his fair chance and made the most of it.

00:12:10.781 --> 00:12:14.381
Despite the handicap of his late start in the accounting business,

00:12:14.381 --> 00:12:20.181
Ed makes good at his job and becomes well-liked by his fellow employees and his employer.

00:12:20.181 --> 00:12:25.241
The Evans home once more becomes a popular meeting place for all of Dorothy's young friends,

00:12:25.241 --> 00:12:27.761
and Johnny in particular.

00:12:27.761 --> 00:12:33.461
Dorothy's fears that she might have to leave school and help support the family have been dispelled,

00:12:33.461 --> 00:12:36.541
and things seem to be working out well on all fronts.

00:12:37.041 --> 00:12:41.641
Through his new acquaintances, Ed's friendships soon begin to multiply,

00:12:41.641 --> 00:12:45.861
and before long, most of his former friends also begin to drift back.

00:12:45.861 --> 00:12:51.901
Once more Ed is able to relax and enjoy himself, knowing that he has fought his way back again,

00:12:51.901 --> 00:12:55.581
able to face the future with a full measure of confidence.

00:12:55.581 --> 00:12:58.441
Returning to the hospital for his regular examination,

00:12:58.441 --> 00:13:06.481
Ed Evans can report that he has been fully rehabilitated in employment, friendship, and family.

00:13:06.491 --> 00:13:10.781
Unfortunately, it has taken too long a time.

00:13:10.781 --> 00:13:14.321
Unfortunately, it had been hard on Ed and his family.

00:13:14.321 --> 00:13:19.241
Needlessly, Ed's former employer lost a valuable worker.

00:13:19.241 --> 00:13:27.001
A lack of knowledge, understanding, and faith has caused these unnecessary losses.

00:13:27.001 --> 00:13:30.101
That has been Ed Evans' story.

00:13:30.101 --> 00:13:32.841
Will this man's be the same?

00:13:32.841 --> 00:13:38.141
His name is Richard Milton, and he has spent nine months, one week, and four days in this hospital.

00:13:38.141 --> 00:13:40.321
Today he has been discharged.

00:13:40.321 --> 00:13:43.361
The disease he had was tuberculosis.

00:13:43.361 --> 00:13:46.701
A decision as to what happens to his future,

00:13:46.701 --> 00:13:51.361
whether he will be received back into an active, full, and worthwhile life

00:13:51.361 --> 00:13:54.921
by his family, his friends, and his employer

00:13:54.921 --> 00:14:00.501
or whether he will be shunned and will have to fight his way back step-by-step

00:14:00.501 --> 00:14:07.701
is something that only one person can say, and that person is you.

00:14:07.701 --> 00:14:24.601
[Music]

